#use(std/code)

#section{Fences}::

Fences are alternative inline-body delimiters for content with unbalanced
braces (Scribble-style). An inline body may be written
#code|={|{ … }|}=| or, when the content itself contains #code|={}|}=|, with
an extended fence #code|={|=={ … }==|}=| — the run of #code{=} in opener and
closer must match. The scan is verbatim: no #code{#} recognition, no brace
counting.

#code(lang: "plain")
	plain #tt|{ a } b }| end
	ext #tt|=={ has }| inside }==| done
	raw #tt{normal} too

Live:

plain #tt|{ a } b }| end
ext #tt|=={ has }| inside }==| done
raw #tt{normal} too

Corner cases:

#list
	#item: Opener #code|={|=={}=| closes only at #code|={}==|}=|, never at #code|={}|}=| — pick a #code{=} run that does not occur in the content.
	#item: Fences work for any inline body kind — markup, raw, or custom — and also in host-mode re-entry: #code|={#(para #tt|{ raw } here }|)}=|.
	#item: Block raw bodies never need fences; an indented raw block is terminated only by dedent, with no delimiter scanning at all.
